import{_ as a,C as s,o as n,c as d,ag as t,E as r}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Sandboxes","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"safeguards/sandbox.md","filePath":"safeguards/sandbox.md"}'),i={name:"safeguards/sandbox.md"};function l(h,e,c,p,f,u){const o=s("SandboxFigure");return n(),d("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t('<h1 id="sandboxes" tabindex="-1">Sandboxes <a class="header-anchor" href="#sandboxes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sandboxes&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A sandbox is a limit that the operating system puts on a process: the files that it can read and write, and the hosts that it can reach. A text check reads what a command says; a sandbox limits what the command does, whatever it says. So the sandbox is the one security boundary of the harness (<a href="./security-model">The security model</a>). This page describes the three sandboxes of the harness, what each one wraps, and the sandboxes that are planned.</p><h2 id="three-sandboxes" tabindex="-1">Three sandboxes <a class="header-anchor" href="#three-sandboxes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Three sandboxes&quot;">​</a></h2>',3)),r(o),e[1]||(e[1]=t('<p><em>The sandboxes of the harness.</em> In a normal session, Claude Code wraps each shell command in its own sandbox; <code>git</code>, <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code> and <code>ps</code> run outside it. The Kaimon server, which a session reaches over MCP, runs under a Seatbelt profile of its own. The launcher <code>agentbox</code> puts one sandbox around a whole frontend, its own process and every command that it starts.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">sandbox</th><th style="text-align:left;">wraps</th><th style="text-align:left;">on</th><th style="text-align:left;">turned on by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="#the-session-sandbox">the session sandbox</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">each shell command of a Claude Code session</td><td style="text-align:left;">macOS, Linux</td><td style="text-align:left;">the settings template; always on</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="#the-launcher-agentbox">the launcher <code>agentbox</code></a></td><td style="text-align:left;">a whole CLI frontend: <code>claude</code>, <code>opencode</code>, <code>omp</code> or <code>agy</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">macOS; on Linux planned</td><td style="text-align:left;">you, by starting the frontend through <code>agentbox</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="#the-daemon-profiles">the daemon profiles</a></td><td style="text-align:left;">the Kaimon server and the <code>julia-update</code> job</td><td style="text-align:left;">macOS; on Linux work in progress</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>harness setup</code>, in the launch agents</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="the-session-sandbox" tabindex="-1">The session sandbox <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-session-sandbox" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The session sandbox&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <code>sandbox</code> section of the settings template turns Claude Code&#39;s sandbox on for every shell command of a session:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">key</th><th style="text-align:left;">value</th><th style="text-align:left;">effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>enabled</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>true</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">each <code>Bash</code> command runs inside the sandbox</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>failIfUnavailable</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>true</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a session does not start when the sandbox cannot start</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>allowUnsandboxedCommands</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>false</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a command cannot ask to run outside the sandbox</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>autoAllowBashIfSandboxed</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>true</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a sandboxed command runs with no prompt; the <a href="./permissions#deny">deny list</a> still applies</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>excludedCommands</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>git</code>, <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code>, <code>ps</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">these run outside the sandbox</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Files.</strong> The write allowlist holds <code>~/Research</code>, <code>~/.julia</code>, the temporary directories, the cache of PoCL, the directories of rtk and a few directories of <code>~/.claude</code>. The write deny list holds the installed files, the settings and the git hooks, and each <code>Edit</code> deny of the permission lists. The read deny list holds the files of keys and tokens, and each <code>Read</code> deny.</p><p><strong>Network.</strong> The allowlist is strict: a host that is not on it is refused. <code>settings/network.toml</code> lists the hosts in three groups, and the profile can add its own. A fourth key, <code>deny</code>, names a host that reaches neither:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">group</th><th style="text-align:left;">reaches the host</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>allow</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the <code>WebFetch</code> tool and a sandboxed command</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>fetch</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the <code>WebFetch</code> tool only: a host that accepts writes, which a session only reads</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>infrastructure</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a sandboxed command only, such as a package server</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Why four commands run outside it.</strong> A sandboxed <code>git</code> cannot write <code>.git</code>, and a sandboxed <code>gh</code> fails its TLS check with error <code>-26276</code> on macOS. So <code>git</code>, <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code> and <code>ps</code> are excluded. A command runs outside the sandbox only when every part of it starts with one of the four and no part redirects to or from a file. <code>cd &lt;other dir&gt; &amp;&amp; git commit</code> would run inside it and fail, so the <a href="./guard-hooks#the-sandbox-check">sandbox check</a> refuses it before it runs. The permission lists and the <a href="./guard-hooks">guard hooks</a> still apply to an excluded command, and <a href="./git-safety">Git safety</a> describes the checks of <code>git</code> and <code>gh</code>.</p><h2 id="the-launcher-agentbox" tabindex="-1">The launcher <code>agentbox</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-launcher-agentbox" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The launcher `agentbox`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The session sandbox wraps the shell commands of Claude Code. It does not wrap Claude Code itself, an MCP server that it starts, or another frontend: OpenCode and oh-my-pi have no sandbox of their own. The launcher <code>agentbox</code> closes this gap. It starts one frontend under <code>srt</code>, the sandbox runtime, so the frontend and everything that it starts run inside one sandbox:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">agentbox</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> claude</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">agentbox</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> opencode</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>agentbox</code> runs <code>~/.local/bin/srt --settings ~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/&lt;policy name&gt;.json -- &lt;program&gt;</code>, where the policy name is the frontend&#39;s name, and <code>antigravity</code> for <code>agy</code>. <code>harness install</code> writes one settings file for each CLI frontend, from the <code>sandbox</code> section of the template and the policy file <code>sandbox/policy.toml</code>, so every frontend gets the same file and network lists. Each frontend gets an argument that fits the outer sandbox:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">frontend</th><th style="text-align:left;">argument</th><th style="text-align:left;">why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>claude</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--settings …/claude-inner-off.json</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Claude Code&#39;s own sandbox fails inside <code>srt</code>, so the inner one is off</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>opencode</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--standalone</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">its server runs as its own child, inside the sandbox</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>omp</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--approval-mode yolo</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">no prompts; the sandbox is the boundary</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The launcher sets <code>AGENTBOX=1</code> and refuses to start inside a launcher. It gives each run new cache directories for npm, uv and LaTeX, because the sandbox denies their caches in the home directory. <code>harness setup</code> installs the pinned <code>srt</code> with <code>npm ci --ignore-scripts</code>.</p><p><strong><code>agentbox</code> is opt-in.</strong> A frontend that you start without it does not read its <code>srt</code> file, and runs as it would without the harness: Claude Code with its session sandbox, OpenCode and oh-my-pi with none. The probe battery <code>scripts/probe-agentbox.py</code> checks the boundary on macOS; run it from a plain terminal, not from a session. <a href="./../components/sandbox">The srt settings</a> describes the rendering, the launcher and the probe in full.</p><p><strong>A frontend can read its own credentials.</strong> The sandbox wraps the frontend and its shell together, so it cannot tell them apart. Each frontend&#39;s settings allow reads of its own credential files by name, and deny those of every other frontend (<a href="./security-model#the-limits">The security model</a>).</p><h2 id="the-daemon-profiles" tabindex="-1">The daemon profiles <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-daemon-profiles" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The daemon profiles&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An MCP server runs outside the session&#39;s sandbox: it is a process of its own, for example one that launchd starts, and the code that it runs for a session does not see the session&#39;s lists. The permission lists control which of its tools a session can call, not what the tools do. On macOS the harness therefore runs the two launch agents that run Julia outside every session under a Seatbelt profile of their own: the Kaimon server and the <code>julia-update</code> job.</p><p>The launch agent of Kaimon starts the server with <code>/usr/bin/sandbox-exec -f ~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/kaimon.sb</code>, a Seatbelt profile that <code>harness setup</code> renders from <code>sandbox/daemon.toml</code>. The profile denies every file read and write, then allows reads of <code>~/Research</code>, the Julia depot, juliaup, Kaimon&#39;s configuration, cache and logs, git&#39;s configuration, the per-user temporary and cache directories, the system directories, the root directory <code>/</code> itself, which dyld reads when a process starts, and <code>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/shims</code>, the first directory of the job&#39;s <code>PATH</code>; and writes of <code>~/Research</code>, the depot, Kaimon&#39;s directories, the per-user directories and <code>/private/tmp</code>. It denies writes to <code>~/.julia/bin</code>, <code>~/.julia/config</code>, <code>~/.julia/juliaup</code>, the harness&#39;s <code>githooks/</code>, and <code>.git/hooks</code>, <code>.git/config</code> and <code>.githooks</code> at any depth below <code>~/Research</code>. After these denies it allows one file again, juliaup&#39;s lock file <code>~/.julia/juliaup/.juliaup-lock</code>, because the juliaup launcher that starts Julia opens it for writing at every start and exits when that fails; <code>juliaup.json</code> and the installed Julia versions stay unwritable. The rule allows every write to that one path, so a confined process can also hold the lock, which makes every juliaup command outside the profile wait, or put a symbolic link in the file&#39;s place, which a juliaup command outside the profile then opens for writing.</p><p>Kaimon starts every Julia session on a pseudo-terminal, so the profile allows the operation <code>pseudo-tty</code> and reads, writes and ioctls of <code>/dev/ptmx</code> and of every <code>/dev/ttysNNN</code> (the key <code>pty</code> of the job). Seatbelt cannot tell the job&#39;s own pseudo-terminal from the terminal window of another program of the same user, so a confined process can open the <code>/dev/ttysNNN</code> of any terminal of the user and write text into it, which that terminal shows as output. The rule also allows a change of the device&#39;s mode, which the user owns, so it does not stop a read of that terminal&#39;s input either. Whether such a process can also push input into another terminal with the ioctl <code>TIOCSTI</code> is not checked on macOS 27. The <code>julia-update</code> job has <code>pty = false</code> and no such rule.</p><p>Kaimon&#39;s profile denies an app or document opened through Launch Services, and a mount. It denies an Apple Event to another app twice: <code>(deny appleevent-send)</code>, and a deny of the Mach services <code>com.apple.coreservices.launchservicesd</code> and <code>com.apple.CoreServices.coreservicesd</code>. On macOS 27.2, under the rendered profile and outside every agent sandbox, <code>osascript -e &#39;tell app &quot;Finder&quot; to get name of startup disk&#39;</code> fails with error <code>-54</code>, and with <code>-600</code> when the profile lacks the Mach-service deny; an <code>AESendMessage</code> call that does not go through <code>osascript</code> also fails, with <code>-600</code>. Each rule alone stops the event before the Automation consent of macOS. The probe <code>appleevent</code> of <code>sandbox/probe-daemon.jl</code> sends this event: AppleScript answers <code>name</code> of an application from the script itself and sends no event.</p><p>The profile leaves open the network, the other Mach services, which Metal needs, every write inside <code>~/Research</code> outside the denied repository parts, and the rename of a directory above a denied part, which moves the part out of the denied path. The writes inside <code>~/Research</code> include <code>~/Research/.claude/hooks</code>, <code>skills</code> and <code>settings.json</code>, which the session sandbox denies: through Kaimon&#39;s <code>ex</code>, code can write a hook that a later session runs outside every sandbox.</p><p>The exec of the setuid <code>/bin/ps</code> fails under the profile, and Kaimon reads a failed <code>ps -o state= -p PID</code> as a dead session, so the shims directory holds a <code>ps</code> of the harness that answers that form with <code>kill -0</code>. It says that any process that <code>kill -0</code> can signal exists, which the job could already learn with <code>kill -0</code> itself, counts a zombie as alive, and counts a process that <code>kill -0</code> may not signal, such as one of another user, as gone. The <code>julia-update</code> job runs under its own profile from the same file. For every other MCP server, no profile applies.</p><p><strong>On Linux</strong> the units of the two jobs run unwrapped. <strong>Work in progress:</strong> a wrapper that runs each unit under <code>bwrap</code>, with the same file rules.</p><h2 id="planned" tabindex="-1">Planned <a class="header-anchor" href="#planned" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Planned&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These sandboxes are decided, and none of them runs yet:</p><ul><li><strong>A guard against a session with no sandbox</strong> (planned): a hook that refuses every tool call when <code>~/.ssh</code> is readable and no sandbox is on: neither the launcher nor Claude Code&#39;s own sandbox.</li><li><strong>Fewer prompts under the launcher</strong> (planned): the permission prompts that the outer sandbox makes safe go, as a settings change with probes.</li><li><strong>The desktop apps</strong> (planned): an outer sandbox for Claude Code&#39;s Code tab and the Antigravity desktop app, which <code>agentbox</code> cannot start, with the same probe battery.</li><li><strong>A Linux wrapper</strong> (planned): the outer sandbox on Linux, tried first on one test machine, then the same battery and a GPU kernel on each Linux machine.</li><li><strong>Masked keys</strong> (planned): the API keys stay in their denied files, and the launcher gives them to <code>srt</code> as masked variables; the <code>gh</code> and <code>glab</code> tokens leave the key store for masked variables too.</li><li><strong>A token per owner</strong> (planned, waits for your tokens): a fine-grained forge token for each organisation, so that a command can write only where the task needs it.</li><li><strong>Kaimon without the network</strong> (planned, waits for the daemon wrapper on Linux): the Kaimon server reaches only the package servers.</li><li><strong>A credential proxy</strong> (planned, optional and last): a proxy that writes the keys into the requests for their named hosts, so that the agent sees only a stand-in. <code>srt</code> already filters the network by host, so the proxy does not run by default.</li></ul>',28))])}const b=a(i,[["render",l]]);export{m as __pageData,b as default};
